@namespace LineOps.Desk.Primitives

@*
    A tooltip: a sentence of explanation that appears beside the thing it explains.

    For the explanation a native title attribute cannot carry — more than a line, a line break
    that matters, a measure that keeps a sentence readable. Everywhere one short phrase will do,
    title stays: it costs nothing, and a component per cell of a large grid would cost a great
    deal for the same words.

    It wraps MudTooltip rather than drawing its own, the way DeskButton wraps MudButton, and for
    the same kind of reason. css/mud-bridge.css already themes .mud-tooltip in the desk's tokens,
    and the behaviour the HIG asks of a tooltip — raised by hover and by keyboard focus alike,
    after a short pause so a passing pointer does not flash it, held to a measure, placed so it
    stays on screen — is exactly what MudBlazor supplies. Drawing it again would be a second
    copy of that behaviour to keep right.

    The tooltip describes its trigger and never renames it: whatever the wrapped control is
    called (its text, its aria-label) is still what a screen reader announces. Text is a plain
    string and the only content API, so what the reader will be told is reachable by a test and
    by the stylesheet alike, and a tooltip cannot quietly grow controls of its own.
*@

<MudTooltip Text="@Text"
            Placement="@Mapped"
            Arrow="true"
            Delay="300"
            ShowOnFocus="true"
            ShowOnHover="true">
    @ChildContent
</MudTooltip>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// What the reader is told, in sentences. A line break in it is kept: two short lines read
    /// better than one long one.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Text { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The trigger — the control or the text the tooltip explains.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which side of the trigger it opens on. Above unless that would be clipped.</summary>
    [Parameter] public DeskTooltipPlacement Placement { get; set; } = DeskTooltipPlacement.Top;

    private MudBlazor.Placement Mapped => Placement switch
    {
        DeskTooltipPlacement.Bottom => MudBlazor.Placement.Bottom,
        DeskTooltipPlacement.Start => MudBlazor.Placement.Start,
        DeskTooltipPlacement.End => MudBlazor.Placement.End,
        _ => MudBlazor.Placement.Top
    };
}
